(4865664, 704442, 51538, '<td valign="top">Indian Pipe (the plant in the background)is, actually, not a member of the fungi group.  It is, rather, a <a href="//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monotropa_uniflora" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">herbaceous perennial plant</a> but lacks the ability to photosynthesize its own food.  It gets nutrition from trees in a parasitic manner.    The only reason I know this is because I photographed some Indian Pipe in Vermont a week or two back and looked up the botany of the plant to make sure.  I too thought it was fungi of some kind.  I like the macro.</td>', 'Indian Pipe (the plant in the background)is, actually, not a member of the fungi group.  It is, rather, a herbaceous perennial plant but lacks the ability to photosynthesize its own food.  It gets nutrition from trees in a parasitic manner.    The only reason I know this is because I photographed some Indian Pipe in Vermont a week or two back and looked up the botany of the plant to make sure.  I too thought it was fungi of some kind.  I like the macro.', '2008-08-04 23:29:45.000000', NULL, 1),
